  acquisition can deadlock and cannot prove that every mutation joined the same
  serialization boundary.


  The reusable coordinator is now implemented. Existing blob and pair
  constructors preserve their legacy sibling locks, while explicit
  `with_coordination(...)` constructors bind either adapter to one caller-owned
  lock domain. Guards are non-cloneable and retain exact lock-path identity.

  Two host-real cases cover shared-guard identity and unchanged blob/pair
  storage behavior under one coordinator.

  Guarded prelocked operations are now implemented for bounded blob load/CAS and
  pair-generation reclamation. Each requires one matching exclusive guard and
  rejects foreign guard identity before state access or deletion. One host-real
  case spans blob CAS/load and pair reclamation under one acquisition; another
  proves both adapters reject a foreign guard.

  Journal-driven reclamation is now implemented under that shared guard. Missing
  journal state performs no deletion, while a present canonical journal supplies
  the exact preservation set for one reclamation pass. Two host-real cases cover
  missing-journal safety and exact durable preservation.

  A separate one-shot transition now CASes the canonical retention journal,
  confirms journal durability, and only then reclaims using the replacement
  retention under the same guard. Conflict cannot delete; journal durability
  failure stops before cleanup, and durable journal publication plus reclamation
  rejection remains committed journal evidence. Three host-real cases cover
  successful cleanup, stale conflict with zero deletion, and durable journal
  retention after reclamation rejection.

  Caller-directed bounded retry now wraps that transition. Only prepublication
  journal conflict retries; each typed conflict becomes the next reconciliation
  callback input, while exhausted conflict remains terminal evidence. Callback
  failure and every committed journal or reclamation outcome stop immediately;
  merge, ordering, backoff, and retention policy remain caller-owned.


  A shared policy-neutral retry directive now lets this retention/reclamation
  loop consult the caller between retryable conflicts. The hook sees the exact
  completed-attempt count after the filesystem guard is released and may return
  `Continue` or `Stop`; stopping preserves the conflict unchanged. Callers may
  synchronously wait, yield, or inspect cancellation there, while timing,
  backoff intervals, fairness, and asynchronous scheduling remain outside the
  runtime.


  Shared retry control now also centralizes exact attempt accounting for these
  synchronous loops. A private cursor starts after the first one-shot attempt,
  supplies the completed-attempt evidence seen by control hooks, and advances
  only while the caller-selected positive maximum permits it. Two focused cases
  cover exact advancement and attempt-limit exhaustion; no timing policy is
  added.


  Terminal retry evidence is centralized at the same boundary. One generic
  container owns exact attempts plus the subsystem terminal outcome, while the
  ordered-count, ordered-pair, latency, retention-journal, and guarded
  reclamation APIs keep their existing public names as aliases. One focused case
  covers borrowed and consuming access with no outcome-policy change.


  Callers may also retain an opaque typed stop reason without changing retry
  loops. A generic decision carries `Continue` or `Stop(reason)`, and a small
  stop-state adapter maps that decision to the existing runtime directive while
  preserving the exact triggering conflict and caller reason. The runtime owns
  no cancellation/yield taxonomy and never interprets that reason. Two focused
  cases cover reasonless continuation and typed stop retention.

  Cached-cycle composition now binds the opaque pair revision to typed count and
  latency owners. Versioned restore decodes both canonical members together and
  retains their exact revision. One-shot durable typed CAS preserves expected
  revision evidence and decodes bounded conflicts back into exact live owners.

  Six focused cases cover typed versioned restore, missing initialization, stale
  conflict decoding, malformed conflict rejection, committed durability failure,
  and host-real cross-instance revision advance. Blind conflict retry remains
  intentionally absent because replaying one stale whole-pair snapshot could
  overwrite concurrent telemetry; retry requires explicit fresh-state
  reconciliation or merge semantics first.

  A separate ordered telemetry-pair contract now persists the existing canonical
  `(external order + count FIFO)` document as member one and canonical latency
  as
  member two. It does not reinterpret or migrate the earlier plain count-window
  pair format. The durable watermark now exists beside latency evidence in one
  atomic publication, enabling safe future reconciliation design.

  Four focused cases cover atomic ordered-pair round trip, missing state,
  committed durability failure, and host-real cross-instance restore.
  Persistence itself still infers no order from storage contention.

  One-shot ordered-pair reconciliation now performs a fresh versioned load,
  transactionally appends one caller-ordered count batch, exactly normalizes and
  merges its latency delta, then performs one durable revision CAS. Missing
  state
  uses caller capacity and submitted latency; persisted capacity and watermark
  remain authoritative after initialization.

  Four focused cases cover initialization, exact existing-state reconciliation,
  stale-order rejection before CAS, and committed durability failure. Conflict
  remains typed non-mutating one-shot evidence.

  Caller-bounded synchronous retry now repeats the complete fresh-load
  reconciliation only after typed CAS conflict. Every attempt re-reads the
  durable watermark and latency state; a newer watermark can reject the
  submitted order rather than being silently rebased. Durable publication,
  committed sync
  failure, and every non-conflict error stop immediately. No timing, fairness,
  cancellation, or backoff policy is inferred.

  Four retry cases cover attempt-limit conflict, conflict-to-fresh-load success,
  conflict followed by exact stale-order rejection, and committed durability
  failure stopping after the first attempt.


  Ordered-pair retry now also accepts the shared synchronous retry directive.
  After a retryable conflict, the caller sees the exact completed-attempt count
  and may return `Continue` or `Stop`; stopping preserves that typed conflict.
  Continued attempts still reload and reconcile the complete pair, while the
  runtime performs no clock read, wait, backoff, or fairness action. Two focused
  cases cover one continued conflict and one caller-stopped conflict.

  The concrete filesystem adapter is likewise payload-neutral. It probes beyond
  bounded reads, stages beside one explicit destination, synchronizes staged
  bytes, and publishes by `rename` without deleting prior state first. Host-real
  cases retain missing, overwrite-or-safe-failure, oversized-read, and exact
  count/latency evidence while permitting other typed composition clients.

  A separate durability capability now confirms the destination directory only
  after replacement commits. The application reports either confirmed
  `Durable` state or committed `Published` state with exact sync failure, so
  post-publication failure is never mislabeled rollback. Two deterministic
  cases cover both outcomes and one host-real case exercises directory sync.

  Cached-cycle composition now binds both canonical count-window and latency
  histogram codecs to the durability-capable application service. Three
  adapter-neutral cases cover typed count/latency success and committed sync
  failure, while one host-real count-window case crosses the full persistence
  stack.

  A transport-neutral monotonic interval port now delimits caller-owned cached
  retry work without exposing wall-clock time. A standard `Instant` adapter owns
  exact elapsed-nanosecond conversion, and cached-cycle composition turns one
  finished interval into the existing explicit latency sample without recording
  it automatically. Two deterministic cases cover exact sampling and finish
  failure; one host-real case records a measured sample separately.

  Merge backoff/cancellation/fairness policy, async queues/timers, reclamation
  scheduling/automatic durable retention policy, general N-object
  transactions, and broader
  production orchestration remain pending.
  Persistent sequence ownership now preloads all exact mappings, reuses them
  across calls, rolls back partial loads, and releases every mapping with retry
  retention. Full chain identity is checked before buffer mutation; adapters own
  unique non-overlapping live allocations.

  One Linux x86-64 host-real case now loads the retained rotate/output chain
  into two distinct mappings owned by the concrete process worker, applies both
  steps exactly, and releases the aggregate sequence through that same child.

  A weighted exact-key FIFO reuses complete loaded chains without refreshing
  hits. Positive limits cover entries
  and can also cover live mappings plus admitted mapped bytes. Candidate weight
  comes from ready mapping reports; oversized candidates clean themselves, while
  fitting candidates evict repeatedly until every projected limit fits. Failed
  later insertion eviction returns failed victim/candidate cleanup ownership.

  Invalidation and full drain update usage before release. Weighted limits now
  reconfigure in place: expansion or already-fitting requests publish without
  adapter work, while shrink requests release oldest entries before publishing.
  Previous limits remain active through failure; removed keys and failed release
  ownership remain explicit for retry, and a repeated request does not release
  completed victims again. Seventeen cases cover original reuse/admission plus
  expansion, entry/mapping/byte shrink, and second-eviction failure. A separate
  immutable `Arc` lease cache now lets exact ready chains remain usable across

  threads after active lookup retirement. Retired entries keep exact weighted
  usage charged until explicit return/reconciliation observes no external
  owner. Mixed eviction releases unleased entries, retires leased entries, and
  rejects candidates blocked by resident weight. Invalidation and full drain
  use the same ownership rule; keyed cleanup failures remain retryable.
  Resident limits now expand without adapter work and shrink through active FIFO

  release/retirement. Leased victims keep exact weight charged, prior retired
  entries are not reconciled implicitly, and old limits survive blockage or
  cleanup failure. Final lease return or keyed retry permits idempotent repeated
  publication. Fourteen cases cover the original lifecycle plus entry/mapping
  shrink, live entry/byte blockage, post-return publication, and release-failure
  retry. Durable or cross-process leasing, fused objects, direct branches, and a

  host-real AArch64 and Windows call paths are non-blocking compatibility
  follow-ups.
  The reviewed forty-nine-shape fused two-step emission matrix is complete.
  Concrete Linux x86-64 executable-memory, instruction-cache, foreign-call,
  and fused whole-region integration now exists through the process worker.
  Broader host coverage remains pending for this TODO.
